1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,073
One question is never enough.

2
00:00:03,393 --> 00:00:09,050
Real analysts ask many, adaptively,
each chosen after seeing earlier answers.

3
00:00:09,370 --> 00:00:12,452
Maybe you spot a spike in one bin, and zoom in.

4
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:15,090
The paper's Theorem 1 handles this.

5
00:00:15,410 --> 00:00:19,919
Write the probability of the whole transcript,
as a product, step by step.

6
00:00:20,239 --> 00:00:24,596
The analyst's choice of the next question
is the same in both worlds,

7
00:00:24,596 --> 00:00:28,700
so in the ratio it cancels,
leaving one Laplace ratio per answer.

8
00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:33,203
If you know reinforcement learning,
this is the trajectory-ratio trick:

9
00:00:33,203 --> 00:00:37,976
whatever is identical in both worlds cancels,
and the per-step log ratios add up.

10
00:00:38,217 --> 00:00:44,299
So privacy losses add up,
and ε behaves like a budget.

11
00:00:44,619 --> 00:00:49,142
Each answer spends part of it;
once it is spent, the curator stops.

12
00:00:49,462 --> 00:00:51,692
That is the answer to Dinur and Nissim:

13
00:00:51,692 --> 00:00:55,352
the budget makes the limit on questions
explicit and measurable.

14
00:00:56,900 --> 00:01:00,006
You can now compute a real saving yourself.

15
00:01:00,326 --> 00:01:05,030
Pause: a histogram with d bins, total budget ε.

16
00:01:05,350 --> 00:01:09,563
Treat each bin as its own counting query
and split the budget evenly.

17
00:01:09,883 --> 00:01:11,694
How much noise does each bin get?

18
00:01:24,533 --> 00:01:29,950
Each bin gets 1/d of the budget,
so noise of scale d/ε.

19
00:01:30,270 --> 00:01:37,610
Treated as one query with sensitivity 2,
each bin gets 2/ε, whatever d is.

20
00:01:37,930 --> 00:01:42,422
The earlier framework's sharper analysis
got this down to about √d,

21
00:01:42,422 --> 00:01:44,098
but it still grew with d.

22
00:01:45,433 --> 00:01:48,057
Sensitivity reaches far beyond counts.

23
00:01:48,377 --> 00:01:50,193
Three examples from the paper.

24
00:01:50,883 --> 00:01:52,838
First, picture a social network

25
00:01:52,838 --> 00:01:56,117
where each possible link
is one row of the database.

26
00:01:56,437 --> 00:02:00,039
How many links must you cut
to split the network in two?

27
00:02:00,359 --> 00:02:05,684
That minimum cut moves by at most 1
when one link changes, so it is 1-sensitive.

28
00:02:06,004 --> 00:02:08,296
In general, any question of the form,

29
00:02:08,296 --> 00:02:13,313
how many rows would you have to change
to make something true, has sensitivity 1.

30
00:02:13,550 --> 00:02:18,510
Second: if an algorithm
that rarely looks at any particular row,

31
00:02:18,510 --> 00:02:25,408
like one working from a small random sample,
approximates f to within σ most of the time,

32
00:02:25,408 --> 00:02:29,515
on every database,
then f has sensitivity at most 2σ.

33
00:02:29,835 --> 00:02:31,116
That is Lemma 1.

34
00:02:31,166 --> 00:02:33,995
Third, the answer need not be a number:

35
00:02:33,995 --> 00:02:39,578
a ranking, a set, a string of bits,
anything with a distance between answers.

36
00:02:39,898 --> 00:02:43,267
Pick an output with probability
that decays exponentially

37
00:02:43,267 --> 00:02:48,112
with its distance from the true answer,
at a rate of ε over twice the sensitivity.

38
00:02:48,432 --> 00:02:53,771
For bit strings, that flips each bit
with probability a little below 1/2:

39
00:02:53,771 --> 00:02:56,916
Warner's coin again, applied to the answer.

40
00:02:59,767 --> 00:03:01,972
Now the last part of the paper.

41
00:03:02,292 --> 00:03:07,470
Statisticians and data miners traditionally
prefer the non-interactive model:

42
00:03:07,470 --> 00:03:12,044
sanitize the data once, publish it,
and let anyone compute anything.

43
00:03:12,364 --> 00:03:14,405
Can that work under this definition?

44
00:03:14,650 --> 00:03:16,722
The paper proves a striking limit.

45
00:03:17,042 --> 00:03:19,935
Let each row be a string of d bits.

46
00:03:20,255 --> 00:03:25,219
Here is a family of simple counting queries:
give each row its own mask,

47
00:03:25,219 --> 00:03:27,031
a subset of bit positions,

48
00:03:27,031 --> 00:03:31,493
and count the rows
with an odd number of ones inside their mask.

49
00:03:31,813 --> 00:03:34,084
Each query has sensitivity 1,

50
00:03:34,084 --> 00:03:40,116
so an interactive curator can answer
any one of them with noise of about 1/ε.

51
00:03:40,350 --> 00:03:44,301
But a one-shot release must prepare
for all of them at once.

52
00:03:44,621 --> 00:03:48,753
Theorem 3 shows
that for at least 2/3 of these queries,

53
00:03:48,753 --> 00:03:52,886
the release looks almost the same
for a random database

54
00:03:52,886 --> 00:03:56,417
where every row has even parity, true answer 0,

55
00:03:56,417 --> 00:04:00,099
as for one
where every row is odd, true answer n.

56
00:04:00,419 --> 00:04:04,191
The most extreme difference possible,
and it cannot be seen,

57
00:04:04,191 --> 00:04:06,895
unless the database is exponentially large:

58
00:04:06,895 --> 00:04:11,171
roughly, every 4 extra bits per row
doubles the rows you would need.

59
00:04:11,417 --> 00:04:13,675
Pause and ponder: couldn't an analyst

60
00:04:13,675 --> 00:04:17,215
just ask the interactive curator
all of these queries too?

61
00:04:28,050 --> 00:04:29,885
No: the budget would run out.

62
00:04:30,205 --> 00:04:35,550
The curator only has to answer the few questions
someone actually asks, chosen later.

63
00:04:35,870 --> 00:04:39,166
A published release cannot know
which few those will be,

64
00:04:39,166 --> 00:04:41,757
so it must be ready for all of them at once.

65
00:04:42,077 --> 00:04:44,011
That is where it breaks.

66
00:04:44,250 --> 00:04:46,335
The proof idea uses two facts.

67
00:04:46,655 --> 00:04:51,213
Privacy forces the release
to treat every possible row almost alike.

68
00:04:51,533 --> 00:04:56,712
And a random mask splits all rows
into two halves mixed like salt and pepper,

69
00:04:56,712 --> 00:05:00,278
so the even half
is like a random poll of everything.

70
00:05:00,598 --> 00:05:04,917
Then the chain trick:
start from completely random rows,

71
00:05:04,917 --> 00:05:08,156
and swap them one at a time for even ones.

72
00:05:08,476 --> 00:05:12,252
Each swap barely moves the output,
and unless n is huge,

73
00:05:12,252 --> 00:05:14,680
all n swaps together barely move it.

74
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:20,010
The same goes for odd rows,
so both databases look like random rows.

75
00:05:20,250 --> 00:05:22,758
Warner's coin, randomized response,

76
00:05:22,758 --> 00:05:27,847
where each person scrambles their own row
so nobody holds the raw data,

77
00:05:27,847 --> 00:05:29,447
is even more limited.

78
00:05:29,672 --> 00:05:33,380
Proposition 2:
even when every row uses the same mask,

79
00:05:33,380 --> 00:05:38,873
for most masks it cannot estimate the odd count
unless n is exponentially large.

80
00:05:39,117 --> 00:05:40,356
Careful with the quantifiers.

81
00:05:40,406 --> 00:05:44,834
For any one query known in advance,
a one-shot release can answer it well.

82
00:05:45,154 --> 00:05:49,654
What is impossible,
unless the database is exponentially large,

83
00:05:49,654 --> 00:05:53,154
is one private release
that answers most of them.

84
00:05:53,474 --> 00:05:57,954
The lesson: for broad, flexible accuracy
with strong privacy,

85
00:05:57,954 --> 00:06:00,085
keep the curator in the loop.

86
00:06:01,733 --> 00:06:05,006
Back to our map,
to see what grew from this paper.

87
00:06:05,326 --> 00:06:09,785
The same year, in an invited paper
titled simply Differential Privacy,

88
00:06:09,785 --> 00:06:12,778
Dwork introduced the name the field still uses.

89
00:06:13,098 --> 00:06:20,004
Another 2006 paper,
with Kenthapadi, McSherry, Mironov and Naor,

90
00:06:20,004 --> 00:06:22,377
added a tiny slack, δ.

91
00:06:22,697 --> 00:06:26,105
Remember the Gaussian
whose ratio escaped the band in the tails?

92
00:06:26,425 --> 00:06:30,459
δ pays for those rare tails,
and lets the bell curve back in.

93
00:06:30,700 --> 00:06:35,510
Scoring every possible answer became
McSherry and Talwar's exponential mechanism.

94
00:06:35,830 --> 00:06:39,350
The privacy budget grew
into a theory of composition,

95
00:06:39,350 --> 00:06:43,799
with a surprise from Dwork, Rothblum and Vadhan:
allow that tiny δ,

96
00:06:43,799 --> 00:06:48,514
and the total loss of k questions
grows only like the square root of k.

97
00:06:48,750 --> 00:06:53,176
Warner's coin,
the model Section 4 showed is most limited,

98
00:06:53,176 --> 00:06:56,762
is what we now call local differential privacy,

99
00:06:56,762 --> 00:07:00,501
used by Google in Chrome
and by Apple on iPhones.

100
00:07:00,821 --> 00:07:03,502
Why the weakest model?

101
00:07:03,822 --> 00:07:08,096
Nobody has to be trusted with the raw data,
and with millions of users,

102
00:07:08,096 --> 00:07:10,804
a few simple statistics can afford the noise.

103
00:07:11,050 --> 00:07:16,895
In 2016, Abadi and colleagues made it practical
to train deep networks privately:

104
00:07:16,895 --> 00:07:18,987
clip each example's gradient,

105
00:07:18,987 --> 00:07:22,595
like our income cap,
which bounds its sensitivity;

106
00:07:22,595 --> 00:07:27,284
add Gaussian noise;
and track the budget over thousands of steps.

107
00:07:27,604 --> 00:07:31,298
If you train models on patient data,
this is your entry point.

108
00:07:31,618 --> 00:07:35,410
A trained network is just another f(x),
and attacks that ask

109
00:07:35,410 --> 00:07:40,466
whether a patient was in the training set
are our subtraction attack, scaled up.

110
00:07:40,700 --> 00:07:42,372
And for the 2020 census,

111
00:07:42,372 --> 00:07:47,807
the US Census Bureau protected
its published tables with differential privacy;

112
00:07:47,807 --> 00:07:51,569
only a few counts,
like state populations, were exact.

113
00:07:51,889 --> 00:07:53,489
But that is a one-shot release.

114
00:07:53,644 --> 00:07:57,494
Pause and ponder: does Section 4 forbid it?

115
00:08:06,333 --> 00:08:12,915
No. The Census tuned its release
for a fixed set of tables chosen in advance.

116
00:08:13,235 --> 00:08:15,321
Section 4 only rules out one release

117
00:08:15,321 --> 00:08:18,565
that is accurate
for most of a huge family of questions.

118
00:08:21,900 --> 00:08:23,338
Let's recap.

119
00:08:23,388 --> 00:08:26,711
One: privacy means changing any one person's row

120
00:08:26,711 --> 00:08:31,210
changes the probability of any output
by at most a factor of e^ε.

121
00:08:31,530 --> 00:08:36,677
Two: a query's sensitivity
is the most one row can change its answer.

122
00:08:36,997 --> 00:08:42,607
Three: Laplace noise
with scale sensitivity/ε makes it private,

123
00:08:42,607 --> 00:08:46,881
with error that does not grow with the database.

124
00:08:47,201 --> 00:08:52,354
Four: one private published table
cannot answer most simple parity counts

125
00:08:52,354 --> 00:08:55,389
unless the database is exponentially large.

126
00:08:56,033 --> 00:09:00,599
Some questions to test yourself;
pause after each.

127
00:09:00,919 --> 00:09:05,459
First: what is the sensitivity
of the average of n numbers between 0 and 1?

128
00:09:13,700 --> 00:09:18,697
Second: in a medical imaging dataset,
each patient contributes 50 slices,

129
00:09:18,697 --> 00:09:21,914
and each slice's gradient is clipped to size C.

130
00:09:22,234 --> 00:09:25,294
How much can one patient
change the summed gradient?

131
00:09:35,533 --> 00:09:39,763
Third: why does the median give
the Laplace mechanism trouble?

132
00:09:40,083 --> 00:09:42,854
Answers, and more exercises,
are in the companion notes.

133
00:09:51,100 --> 00:09:55,473
Next time someone says a dataset is safe
because it is anonymized,

134
00:09:55,473 --> 00:09:57,792
you will know the better questions:

135
00:09:57,792 --> 00:10:01,171
what is the ε,
and what counts as one person's row?
